&lt;p&gt;Join us for a fascinating discussion with &lt;strong&gt;Andrew Zimmern&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Barton Seaver&lt;/strong&gt;, co-authors of T&lt;em&gt;he Blue Food Cookbook &amp;ndash; Delicious Seafood Recipes for a Sustainable Future&lt;/em&gt;, facilitated by &lt;strong&gt;Sandra Goldmark&lt;/strong&gt;, the Climate School&amp;rsquo;s Dean for Impact and Engagement.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An Emmy- and James Beard Award-winning television host, chef, and food advocate, &lt;strong&gt;Zimmern&lt;/strong&gt; joins &lt;strong&gt;Seaver&lt;/strong&gt;, a leading seafood sustainability expert, to explore how &amp;ldquo;blue foods&amp;rdquo; &amp;mdash; fish, shellfish, and other aquatic life &amp;mdash; can help create a more sustainable, equitable, and delicious future.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Blue Food Cookbook&lt;/em&gt; is more than a cookbook. It&amp;rsquo;s a guide to understanding and protecting our relationship with the world&amp;rsquo;s waters. As the authors write, &amp;ldquo;This is not just a collection of recipes. It&amp;rsquo;s a narrative about why seafood matters, how it has sustained cultures for millennia, and why we need to protect and enhance it for generations to come.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;

